Acute Eye Injury
Acute Urgent Care
Severe ocular pain, Reduced visual acuity, Photophobia, Positive fluorescein stain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-006
Reason for Visit: Severe, sudden-onset eye pain and vision loss requiring immediate clinical assessment to prevent permanent ocular damage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, severe unilateral ocular pain with sudden reduction in visual acuity and extreme photophobia following a mechanical injury. Urgent assessment was required to rule out a penetrating globe injury or severe corneal abrasion and initiate immediate treatment, as delaying until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of permanent vision loss and secondary infection.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: It feels like there is a shard of glass scraping my eye every time I blink, and everything is blurry.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports severe unilateral ocular pain that began abruptly 2 hours ago while sanding wood without safety goggles. The pain was initially a gritty sensation but rapidly escalated to sharp, stabbing pain accompanied by a sudden reduction in visual acuity in the right eye. The patient describes extreme photophobia, requiring the room lights to be kept off. There is profuse reflex tearing and blepharospasm making it difficult to keep the eye open voluntarily.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to navigate safely due to severe photophobia and eye pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living environment has poor ambient lighting; high risk of tripping due to acute vision deficit.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient is in moderate distress, sitting with eyes tightly shut and shielding the face from ambient light.
Physical Exam: The right eye exhibits marked conjunctival injection and a prominent ciliary flush. No gross foreign body is visible on the cornea or under the everted upper eyelid. The pupil is round and reactive, without the irregularity or peaking that would indicate a penetrating injury. There is no hyphaema or macroscopic blood in the anterior chamber. However, the diagnostic tetrad of severe ocular pain, reduced visual acuity, and photophobia is prominent. An expected positive fluorescein stain under a cobalt blue light would reveal a large corneal epithelial defect, confirming the diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: Wood dust on the patient's clothing; safety glasses are absent from the immediate area.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular eye drops or systemic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Eye Injury - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to normal pupil size and reactivity, absence of nausea/vomiting, and a clear history of mechanical trauma.

Penetrating Globe Injury - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a teardrop-shaped pupil, no prolapsed iris tissue, and an intact anterior chamber without hyphaema.

Viral Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the sudden traumatic onset, unilateral severe pain, and absence of an upper respiratory prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's clinical presentation is highly concerning for a significant corneal abrasion or retained microscopic foreign body, evidenced by the severe ocular pain, reflex tearing, and photophobia following high-velocity particulate exposure. The urgent after-hours home assessment was entirely justified because delaying slit-lamp examination and prophylactic antibiotic therapy until daytime consulting hours would expose the patient to a significant risk of infectious keratitis, corneal ulceration, and irreversible vision impairment. Immediate evaluation successfully ruled out a gross penetrating injury while establishing the need for urgent ophthalmological or emergency department equipment to perform definitive fluorescein staining.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Tetracaine 0.5% eye drops stat (single dose only) to facilitate examination. Prescribed Chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops QID as prophylaxis. Referred to local emergency department with eye-care facilities for formal slit-lamp exam.
Positive Behavioral Change: By wearing wrap-around safety goggles every single time you use power tools, you create a physical barrier that prevents high-speed particles from striking your eye, thereby avoiding painful injuries and protecting your long-term vision.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Wrap-around safety goggles strongly recommended for future use.
Follow-up: Handover to the emergency department for definitive imaging and removal of any microscopic foreign body.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department via private vehicle. If the pain suddenly changes in character, or if a dark curtain appears over the vision, seek immediate emergency help.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A full examination of the back of the eye and the clear surface requires specialized equipment at the hospital.
Clinical Course: Corneal abrasions heal rapidly within 24-48 hours with appropriate antibiotic drops, but untreated injuries can quickly become infected ulcers.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep the eye closed and patched lightly on the way to the hospital. Do not rub the eye under any circumstances, as this will drive any retained dust deeper into the clear surface of the eye.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The numbing drop I gave you will wear off in 20 minutes; it was only used to allow me to open your eye safely to check for major damage. The antibiotic drops will prevent a secondary infection.
Actionable Steps: Have your roommate drive you to the emergency department immediately. Keep sunglasses on during the trip to reduce the pain caused by headlights or streetlamps.

